Velvet Rigor Mortis
Ingredients: DISCO + REBEL, ANGRY & AGGRESSIVE + Orchestra + 1950s ERA
Creative Catalyst: It was first performed at a wedding between two rival circus dynasties.
Subverting the glitz of disco into a snarling, militant weapon, this genre trades four-on-the-floor euphoria for a limping, furious waltz-stomp. The 1950s aesthetic is stripped of its suburban innocence and retrofitted with the soot-stained panic of mid-century industrial decay. Sweeping, overly dramatic classical orchestration is weaponized, playing violently out-of-tune melodies that clash against abrasive, distorted rhythm tracks, creating a sound that feels less like a dancefloor and more like a high-wire act over a pit of burning velvet.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 114 BPM; a jagged, lurching triplet-swing that deliberately trips over its own feet before violently correcting itself.
- Harmonic Palette: Diminished orchestral scales drenched in minor-seventh lounge chords, weaponized to induce visceral discomfort.
- Production Signatures: 1) Direct-injection reel-to-reel tape saturation driven past the point of structural collapse, 2) Orchestral mic preamps intentionally short-circuited during heavy brass swells, 3) Sidechain compression triggered exclusively by the snap of a breaking trapeze wire.
- Dynamics: Abrupt, suffocating lulls that suddenly erupt into wall-of-sound brass explosions without warning or transition.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the smoke-choked sawdust of a provincial circus ground at midnight, Velvet Rigor Mortis emerged when the star high-wire acrobat of the Flying Zampinis eloped with the lead lion tamer of the rival Carnivàle Noir. The wedding ceremony—held inside a collapsing canvas tent amidst a torrential downpour—demanded a musical score that could out-shout the roaring lions and the screaming family patriarchs. The subculture's fashion is a terrifying collision of black-tie formalwear torn to shreds by wild beasts, greasepaint smeared with genuine soot, and vintage 1950s spectator shoes caked in dried mud and gunpowder.

Typical Instruments: Bowed contrabass, distorted 1950s archtop electric guitars, live marching-band timpani, aggressively percussive glockenspiels, and modified rotary-dial telephones used as erratic synth-fuzz generators.

For Fans Of: Scott Walker's darkest avant-garde orchestrations, The Birthday Party played on antique brass, Einstürzende Neubauten scoring a 1950s high school prom.

